    "description": "Iran’s national championships are sport-specific competitions with different events, categories and qualification purposes. Athletics, cycling and combat-sport records show why a national title must be read alongside its discipline, participant category and competition date.",
    "tags": ["National championships", "Athletics", "Cycling", "Muaythai", "Competition records", "Team selection"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T07:23:05Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T07:23:05Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "National Championships in Iran | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "How to understand Iranian national titles through documented athletics, road-cycling and Muaythai championships, with careful distinctions between results and selection."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-of-national-title",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A national title needs a category",
        "content": "National championships in Iran are best understood through the rules and records of each sport. An athletics meeting awards results across running, jumping and throwing events; a road-cycling championship distinguishes road races from individual time trials; a combat-sport championship may divide competitors by age and discipline. The label “national champion” therefore needs an accompanying event and participant category. The inspected athletics, cycling and Muaythai records illustrate these differences rather than a single nationwide competition format. [s1][s2][s3][s4]\n\nThis distinction is useful when reading an athlete’s biography. Winning a particular event, leading a team classification and securing national-team selection are different outcomes. A source that documents one does not automatically establish the others. Championship records become more informative when they retain the competition name, category, venue and date instead of reducing an achievement to an unqualified title. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "athletics-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Athletics: performances within a meeting",
        "content": "World Athletics lists Iranian championship meetings at Sardar Jangal Stadium in Rasht in May 2025. Its women’s record is dated 4–5 May and its men’s record 8–9 May. Keeping those dates separate preserves what the inspected result entries actually report. [s1][s2]\n\nThe result sheets also demonstrate why the underlying performance matters. Maryam Toosi heads the women’s 100 metres final, while Hassan Taftian heads the men’s equivalent. Each sprint final includes a wind reading alongside the finishing marks. The record consequently supplies context that a list of winners alone would omit. It also keeps different events separate: a 100 metres result and a 400 metres result describe different contests, even when they belong to the same championship meeting. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "cycling-example",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Cycling: separate races and participant classes",
        "content": "The Union Cycliste Internationale, cycling’s international governing body, lists Iran’s 2025 National Road Championships for 24–25 July with the classification CN, meaning national championships. The entry separates elite and junior categories for women and men, and distinguishes individual road races from individual time trials. [s3]\n\nThose distinctions are essential to interpreting a title. A junior road-race champion should be identified by that category, rather than presented as the winner of every national cycling discipline. The inspected UCI page establishes the listed programme and classification; it does not, on its own, establish who won each race. A calendar entry and a completed result sheet answer different questions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "selection-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Championships and international selection",
        "content": "Some championships also contribute to national-team selection. In an October 2026 report, the International Federation of Muaythai Associations described an Iranian national championship programme in Qom as a qualification and selection platform for forthcoming international events. Its account covered different age groups and several forms of Muaythai, a combat sport with associated technical and cultural disciplines. [s4]\n\nThat report was published while the stated championship period was still underway. It supports an account of the announced programme and selection purpose, but it is not a final results bulletin. Nor does its promotional description independently demonstrate equal access, successful development or guaranteed international selection for every winner. [s4]\n\nFor readers exploring Iranian sport, the most useful approach is therefore precise and sport-specific: establish which championship took place, distinguish an announced programme from completed results, and identify exactly what each title or selection decision represents. The examples here provide that reading framework without claiming to catalogue every national championship in Iran. [s1][s3][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
